{"name":"Bindi Dot","hex":"#8b3439","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bindi","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The “holy dot” traditionally worn on the forehead of Hindu women.","Makeup or jewellery worn in imitation of such a dot."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The common lawn weed, Soliva sessilis, introduced to Australia from South America.","A seed of this plant, having small sharp spines which stick painfully into bare feet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bindi"},{"term":"dot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Dass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, round spot.","A punctuation mark used to indicate the end of a sentence or an abbreviated part of a word; a full stop; a period."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bindi","blocks":[{"text":"A bindi is a coloured dot or, in modern times, a sticker worn on the centre of the forehead, by Hindus, Jains, and Buddhists from the Indian subcontinent. While there are regional variations, traditional bindis are red or maroon, made with material such as kumkum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Kullu_Himachal_Pradesh_India_Woman.jpg/330px-Kullu_Himachal_Pradesh_India_Woman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bindi"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Indian_Dancer_%28Malabika_Sen%29.jpg/500px-Indian_Dancer_%28Malabika_Sen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Indian_Dancer_%28Malabika_Sen%29.jpg/500px-Indian_Dancer_%28Malabika_Sen%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndianDancer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wiki